1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,272
Before you let 11 AI agents run your channel, look at what broke in 7 days.

2
00:00:05,372 --> 00:00:15,744
I tested 11 open-source agent skills designed to handle scriptwriting, hook scoring, title packaging, and edit cuts—with zero manual intervention until publishing.

3
00:00:15,844 --> 00:00:25,579
Today, we're testing the core three in real time on this exact video, and I’m showing you where they save hours versus where they completely hallucinate.

4
00:00:25,679 --> 00:00:31,788
Most creators write a title, then open Photoshop and slap the exact same words on the thumbnail.

5
00:00:31,888 --> 00:00:34,179
That wastes half your click surface.

6
00:00:34,279 --> 00:00:35,479
Watch this.

7
00:00:35,579 --> 00:00:46,841
The package tool runs a Python linter checking two specific rules: character truncation for mobile feeds under 40 characters, and duplicate words across the title and thumbnail.

8
00:00:46,941 --> 00:00:53,877
When I tested a generic title: *"How AI Built My Entire YouTube Channel"*, it flagged it for vague buzzwords.

9
00:00:53,977 --> 00:01:02,503
But when we changed it to: *"11 AI Agents Ran My Entire Channel"* with the thumbnail text *"ZERO HUMANS?"*—it scored a 100 out of 100.

10
00:01:02,603 --> 00:01:06,676
It forces you to make the thumbnail say what the title does not.

11
00:01:06,776 --> 00:01:08,239
Next is `hookscore.py`.

12
00:01:08,339 --> 00:01:11,266
A lot of people think hooks are just gut feel.

13
00:01:11,366 --> 00:01:18,238
This script scores the first 15 seconds against 74 calibrated short-form transcripts across five parameters.

14
00:01:18,338 --> 00:01:19,674
Look at the terminal.

15
00:01:19,774 --> 00:01:26,774
If I input: *"I gave AI full control of my channel and promised not to touch it"*, it scores a 41—marked WEAK.

16
00:01:26,874 --> 00:01:28,074
Why?

17
00:01:28,174 --> 00:01:33,455
Because the curiosity is dead and it doesn't speak to "you" in the first six words.

18
00:01:33,555 --> 00:01:41,954
When we reframe it to: *"Before you let 11 AI agents run your channel, look at what broke in 7 days"*, the score jumps to 74 STRONG.

19
00:01:42,054 --> 00:01:48,290
It forces concrete figures and personal stakes before you waste a single take recording on camera.

20
00:01:48,390 --> 00:01:56,980
Here is where most creators lose 5 hours every weekend: scrubbing through an audio file cutting out 'ums', retakes, and awkward pauses.

21
00:01:57,080 --> 00:02:02,171
Instead of doing that manually, the edit agent parses the raw `.srt` transcript.

22
00:02:02,271 --> 00:02:09,652
It calculates silence thresholds over 800 milliseconds and flags repetitive sentences where you restarted a thought.

23
00:02:09,752 --> 00:02:16,051
It outputs a cut list with exact timecodes that you drop straight into Premiere or DaVinci Resolve.

24
00:02:16,151 --> 00:02:17,615
Now, here is the catch.

25
00:02:17,715 --> 00:02:21,342
The agents cannot run your channel completely unattended.

26
00:02:21,442 --> 00:02:30,859
If you don't feed them a strict `voice.md` profile, they revert right back to generic AI sludge—phrases like *"let's dive in"* and *"game-changer"*.

27
00:02:30,959 --> 00:02:36,749
You still need human taste to kill the fluff, verify the numbers, and actually hit publish.

28
00:02:36,849 --> 00:02:38,049
The skills write.

29
00:02:38,149 --> 00:02:39,349
You upload.

30
00:02:39,449 --> 00:02:46,830
All 11 skills and the 6 standalone Python scripts are 100% free, MIT licensed, with no API keys or signups required.

31
00:02:46,930 --> 00:02:51,257
The GitHub link and setup instructions are in the description below.

32
00:02:51,357 --> 00:02:51,784
Clone the repo, run the hook scorer on your next video, and see what it scores.
